  faqHeading: "Website development in Deira: what trading firms ask first",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What kind of website does a Deira trading company need?", answer: `In most cases a searchable catalogue with an enquiry path, not an online shop. Buyers arrive on item and category pages looking for a code, a size or a quantity, and they finish the conversation on WhatsApp. A catalogue site of up to 100 pages starts from ${P.site}; a generated catalogue covering several thousand lines starts from ${P.seoSite}.` },
    { question: "Can you put my whole price list on the website?", answer: "Yes, and importing it is cheaper than typing it. We need one row per sellable line, a stable item code, a single category per item, pack quantity and unit in their own columns, and a price column containing only numbers. Cleaning a messy sheet is the real work, and it is worth doing once because every later update rides on that structure." },
    { question: "Should wholesale prices be visible to everyone?", answer: "Usually not. The common pattern is a public catalogue with full specifications and an optional indicative price, with the real trade tier revealed only after you approve a buyer account. Hiding every number costs you trust with first-time visitors and some search value, so most firms publish something and keep the margin behind the login." },
    { question: "Do I need a different website because my licence is a general trading licence?", answer: "Not a different website, but a different content check. A general trading licence covers a broad set of goods while a specific commercial licence lists named activity lines, and a catalogue must stay inside whatever yours permits. We write out the product families before building and you confirm them against the licence with your PRO. Some goods need separate registration on top." },
    { question: "Will the site work for buyers who re-export from Dubai?", answer: "That is the main case we design for. HS codes, pack and carton data, weights and dimensions live on item pages; the enquiry form asks for destination port, quantity and Incoterm; and a capability page states which documents you can produce and who attests them. Buyers comparing suppliers treat that stated capability as a shortlisting signal." },
    { question: "Can you build the website in Arabic and Urdu as well as English?", answer: "We build and test both right-to-left layouts, including forms, validation messages, automated emails and the quote document. Urdu needs a Nastaliq typeface and more line height than Arabic, so it is not simply the same pages in another language. We do not write either language; your translator supplies the text and we import it." },
    { question: "How do I take payment if my customers pay cash or by transfer?", answer: "Build for how you are paid now. For wholesale orders the site produces a quote and then an invoice carrying your licensed name, bank details and tax registration, and the buyer transfers. Cards make sense for retail lines or for deposits against a confirmed order, where the gateway is opened in your company's name." },
    { question: "Do you handle the gold rate on a jewellery website?", answer: "We build whichever rate mechanism you choose: a live feed from a source you are licensed to use, displayed with its timestamp and attribution, or a single field your office updates once or twice a day, or no published rate with making charges explained instead. We will not scrape a feed whose terms forbid it, and we do not advise on which option suits your business." },
    { question: "How does VAT work on a catalogue that includes gold and ordinary goods?", answer: "The tax treatment is stored as a field on each item rather than assumed by the checkout, because one catalogue can contain standard-rated lines, zero-rated investment-grade precious metals and business-to-business gold and diamond supplies that fall under a reverse charge. Your accountant decides which class each line sits in and we configure the quote and invoice to match." },
    { question: "Should I sell on Noon and Amazon.ae instead of building my own site?", answer: "Do both, for different parts of the range. Marketplaces reach consumers who want one piece and keep the customer relationship; your own catalogue reaches buyers searching for a code or a quantity and keeps their details with you. Build the catalogue as structured data once and generate the marketplace feed from the same file." },
    { question: "How long does a Deira catalogue site take to build?", answer: "Two to three weeks from an approved quote for a catalogue site, longer for a large generated catalogue, and the delay is almost never code. It is photographs and a clean price list. Firms that start shooting stock and tidying the spreadsheet while the build runs launch on time; firms that wait for the design to be finished do not." },
    { question: "Do you have anyone in Dubai who can visit my shop?", answer: "No. BtechWaleTech is three freelance developers in India with no UAE office, no UAE licence, no UAE phone number and no staff in the country. We cannot photograph stock, verify inventory, collect documents or attend a meeting in Deira. India runs 1.5 hours ahead of Dubai, so messages and calls happen inside your working day." },
    { question: "Will my catalogue rank ahead of the big marketplaces?", answer: `For broad terms, usually not, and nobody can promise a position. Directories and marketplaces hold most first pages for generic commercial queries in this market. What a properly structured catalogue does win is the long tail: specific codes, specific size and material combinations, and sourcing queries naming an area. Ongoing search work starts from ${P.seo}.` },
  ],
